1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:09,000
Lay a loop of string on a ball and pull it tight. It slides off and shrinks to a point.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:16,767
On a doughnut, a loop through the hole gets stuck. No amount of sliding will free it.

3
00:00:16,767 --> 00:00:25,800
In 1904, Henri Poincaré asked whether this simple test can recognise the simplest shape one dimension up.

4
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:33,467
It took a century, and a mathematician named Grigori Perelman, to answer yes.

5
00:00:39,733 --> 00:00:49,733
Topology is the study of shapes that you may stretch and bend as much as you like, but never tear or glue.

6
00:00:49,733 --> 00:00:54,933
To a topologist, a coffee mug and a doughnut are the same shape.

7
00:00:54,933 --> 00:01:02,133
Each has exactly one hole, and one can be squished into the other.

8
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:10,367
But a ball and a doughnut are different. No squishing can make the hole go away.

9
00:01:10,367 --> 00:01:16,867
How can you tell shapes apart if you live inside one and cannot look at it from outside?

10
00:01:16,867 --> 00:01:23,100
Poincaré's answer was a test with a loop of string.

11
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:28,900
Lay a loop anywhere on the shape and pull it tight, keeping it on the surface.

12
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:34,433
On a ball's surface, every loop shrinks to a point.

13
00:01:34,433 --> 00:01:41,100
On a doughnut, a loop around the hole cannot shrink, however you slide it.

14
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:48,033
A shape in which every loop can shrink is called simply connected.

15
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:51,767
For surfaces, this one test is enough.

16
00:01:51,767 --> 00:01:59,600
Every closed surface, finite and with no edge, in which every loop shrinks, is a sphere.

17
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:05,133
That was known by the end of the eighteen hundreds.

18
00:02:05,133 --> 00:02:15,200
So: shapes up to stretching, and one test. Can every loop shrink to a point?

19
00:02:19,433 --> 00:02:21,767
Now go up one dimension.

20
00:02:21,767 --> 00:02:31,433
A closed three-dimensional space looks like ordinary space near every point, but it is finite and has no edge,

21
00:02:31,433 --> 00:02:37,100
like a video game screen where leaving on one side brings you back on the other.

22
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:47,033
You could live inside one, and if you travelled far enough in a straight line, you might come back to where you started.

23
00:02:47,033 --> 00:02:56,167
The simplest such space is built by gluing. Glue two line segments at their ends, and you get a circle.

24
00:02:56,167 --> 00:03:04,400
Glue two discs along their edges, like the two halves of a balloon, and you get a sphere.

25
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:10,133
Glue two solid balls along their surfaces, and you get the three-sphere.

26
00:03:10,133 --> 00:03:16,533
Leave one ball through its surface, and you find yourself inside the other.

27
00:03:16,533 --> 00:03:29,167
It naturally lives in four dimensions, so we cannot draw it, but you could live inside it, and every loop in it can be shrunk.

28
00:03:29,167 --> 00:03:36,000
Poincaré asked: does the lasso test still recognise the sphere, one dimension up?

29
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:46,067
If every loop in a closed three-dimensional space can shrink, must the space be a three-sphere in disguise?

30
00:03:46,067 --> 00:03:53,533
Strangely, higher dimensions turned out to be easier, because there is more room to move things around.

31
00:03:53,533 --> 00:04:03,733
Stephen Smale settled dimensions five and up in 1961, and Michael Freedman dimension four in 1982.

32
00:04:03,733 --> 00:04:10,033
Dimension three, the one Poincaré asked about, resisted every attack.

33
00:04:10,033 --> 00:04:16,833
In 2000 it became one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems.

34
00:04:16,833 --> 00:04:27,400
So the question is: in three dimensions, does every loop shrinking force the shape to be a sphere?

35
00:04:31,633 --> 00:04:42,367
In 1982, Richard Hamilton suggested a new approach. Don't stare at the shape. Let it change.

36
00:04:42,367 --> 00:04:49,800
Give the shape a geometry, with bumps, dents and curvature, and let it smooth itself out over time,

37
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:58,000
the way heat flows from hot spots to cold ones until the temperature is even.

38
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,800
His rule is called Ricci flow.

39
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:07,800
Where the space curves like a sphere, it shrinks; where it curves like a saddle, it expands.

40
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:12,967
It is a heat equation for geometry.

41
00:05:12,967 --> 00:05:16,900
A simpler cousin works on curves in a flat plane.

42
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:24,767
Every point moves toward the inside of its bend, faster where the curve bends more sharply.

43
00:05:24,767 --> 00:05:30,733
Any such curve loses its wiggles, becomes round, and shrinks to a point.

44
00:05:30,733 --> 00:05:38,233
Gage, Hamilton and Grayson proved this in the nineteen eighties.

45
00:05:38,233 --> 00:05:44,900
Hamilton proved the same for three-dimensional shapes that already curve positively in every direction.

46
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:55,067
They become rounder and rounder, and vanish as a round point. So they were spheres all along.

47
00:05:55,067 --> 00:06:04,833
So: let the shape smooth itself by its own curvature, and see what it becomes.

48
00:06:09,066 --> 00:06:18,166
For a general shape, the flow can go wrong. Picture a dumbbell: two balls joined by a thin neck.

49
00:06:18,166 --> 00:06:23,966
The neck is curved tightly around its waist, so the flow squeezes it hardest.

50
00:06:23,966 --> 00:06:29,966
It pinches off in finite time, while the balls are still large.

51
00:06:29,966 --> 00:06:39,700
At that moment the curvature becomes infinite, and the flow cannot go on. This is called a singularity.

52
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:51,433
Hamilton's plan was surgery. Just before a neck pinches, cut it, cap each end with a round cap, and keep flowing.

53
00:06:51,433 --> 00:06:58,666
To make the plan work, three things had to be shown. Every singularity must be one you can cut.

54
00:06:58,666 --> 00:07:07,333
The cuts must not pile up out of control. And the flow must end in a way you can read.

55
00:07:07,333 --> 00:07:16,666
Necks are fine. But a long, thin, cigar-shaped tip that never pinches would be a disaster, with no good place to cut.

56
00:07:16,666 --> 00:07:22,200
Hamilton could not rule it out.

57
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:32,166
Necks pinch, and surgery cuts them. After twenty years, three things were still missing.

58
00:07:36,367 --> 00:07:46,834
In November 2002, Grigori Perelman, a mathematician in Saint Petersburg, posted the first of three papers online.

59
00:07:46,834 --> 00:07:52,734
Together, they finished Hamilton's program.

60
00:07:52,734 --> 00:08:02,700
His first idea was a quantity, built from the geometry, that only goes up while the flow runs, a bit like entropy in physics.

61
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:09,434
Something that can only move one way is a powerful tool.

62
00:08:09,434 --> 00:08:16,500
From it he proved that wherever the curvature is under control, the shape cannot be squashed thin.

63
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:24,734
A small ball always holds a fair share of the volume an ordinary ball would hold.

64
00:08:24,734 --> 00:08:36,100
That kills the cigar. Zoom in on its tip, and you see a squashed, collapsed tube, which the flow can never produce.

65
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:45,300
Second, he showed that wherever the curvature is very large, the shape looks almost exactly like one of a short list of models:

66
00:08:45,300 --> 00:08:53,634
a thin round neck, a neck closed off by a cap, or a small, nearly round piece.

67
00:08:53,634 --> 00:08:59,400
Every one of them is something you know how to cut.

68
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:05,334
Third, he said exactly where to cut, deep inside very thin necks,

69
00:09:05,334 --> 00:09:14,700
and proved that the cuts never pile up: only finitely many happen in any stretch of time.

70
00:09:14,700 --> 00:09:19,367
Finally, for a shape in which every loop shrinks,

71
00:09:19,367 --> 00:09:27,800
he proved that the flow with surgery burns the shape away completely, in finite time.

72
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:34,034
He measured how wide the shape is, using the smallest disc that a loop can be filled with,

73
00:09:34,034 --> 00:09:43,934
and showed that this width shrinks at a steady rate. Something positive that shrinks at a steady rate must reach zero.

74
00:09:43,934 --> 00:09:57,034
So: a quantity that only rises, no collapsing, a short list of shapes, surgery under control, and extinction.

75
00:10:01,266 --> 00:10:06,833
Start with any closed three-dimensional space in which every loop shrinks.

76
00:10:06,833 --> 00:10:16,933
Run the flow, cut each neck before it pinches, and wait. After finitely many cuts, nothing is left.

77
00:10:16,933 --> 00:10:19,566
Now run the film backwards.

78
00:10:19,566 --> 00:10:28,533
Every piece that vanished was a known shape: a round three-sphere, or one of a few close relatives.

79
00:10:28,533 --> 00:10:38,166
Each of those relatives contains a loop that gets stuck, and our shape has none. So every piece was a three-sphere.

80
00:10:38,166 --> 00:10:42,800
Gluing three-spheres together along necks gives back a three-sphere.

81
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:48,933
So the shape we started with was a three-sphere all along.

82
00:10:48,933 --> 00:10:56,566
Flow, cut, burn away, and read the answer backwards.

83
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:04,034
Perelman never sent his papers to a journal.

84
00:11:04,034 --> 00:11:15,334
They were extremely compressed, with many steps left to the expert reader, so others set out to write every step in full.

85
00:11:15,334 --> 00:11:22,934
Bruce Kleiner and John Lott wrote detailed notes. John Morgan and Gang Tian wrote a book-length proof.

86
00:11:22,934 --> 00:11:33,867
Huai-Dong Cao and Xi-Ping Zhu published another account. By 2006, they agreed: the proof was right.

87
00:11:33,867 --> 00:11:41,867
In 2006, Perelman was awarded the Fields Medal, one of the highest honours in mathematics.

88
00:11:41,867 --> 00:11:47,434
He became the first person to decline it.

89
00:11:47,434 --> 00:11:55,367
In 2010, the Clay Institute awarded him the first Millennium Prize, a million dollars.

90
00:11:55,367 --> 00:12:04,667
He declined that too, saying that Hamilton's contribution was no less than his own.

91
00:12:04,667 --> 00:12:12,934
Checked by several teams, and two prizes, both declined.

92
00:12:17,166 --> 00:12:24,666
So the Poincaré conjecture is true. The lasso test recognises the three-sphere.

93
00:12:24,666 --> 00:12:29,166
The same tools prove Thurston's geometrization conjecture:

94
00:12:29,166 --> 00:12:35,400
every closed three-dimensional space is built from eight kinds of uniform pieces,

95
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:41,133
a classification as complete as the one for surfaces.

96
00:12:41,133 --> 00:12:51,166
It does not settle a subtler version in four dimensions, about shapes you can do calculus on, which is still open.

97
00:12:51,166 --> 00:13:02,200
And every complete account runs to hundreds of pages. As far as we know, no computer has checked the whole proof yet.

98
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:11,033
It is still the only Millennium problem the Clay Institute has declared solved.

99
00:13:12,066 --> 00:13:21,233
Let's recap. A test with a loop of string, from 1904. A shape that smooths itself by its own curvature.

100
00:13:21,233 --> 00:13:24,266
Necks cut just before they pinch.

101
00:13:24,266 --> 00:13:32,366
And a shape in which every loop shrinks, burning away to nothing: a three-sphere all along.

102
00:13:32,366 --> 00:13:40,200
A question about pure shape, answered with the mathematics of heat.
